d charcoal (药用炭), as adsorption reduces their bioavailability [Data: Relationships (50043)]. This broad interaction potential suggests that alkaloid-containing herbal products may pose clinically significant drug–herb interaction risks, particularly in polypharmacy settings.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Bilirubin-related indicators confirm hepatobiliary dysfunction in this network\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Both TBIL (total bilirubin) and总胆红素 are used interchangeably as clinical indicators of liver dysfunction within this community. Elevated TBIL levels are reported in association with several entities: SMV (simvastatin?) use shows rare but notable increases [Data: Relationships (104)], while三唑类抗真菌药物 (triazole antifungals) also affect total bilirubin through CYP450-mediated mechanisms [Data: Relationships (180)]. More directly, studies on何首乌 (Polygonum multiflorum) derivatives—including首乌藤 and 黄独素B—show consistent TBIL elevation due to MRP2 inhibition, confirming a cholestatic pattern [Data: Relationships (4935, 5014)]. Thus, bilirubin serves as a convergent biomarker linking diverse agents in this network to hepatobiliary impairment.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Cholestasis is a contraindication for certain drugs and a consequence of others\",\n            \"explanation\": \"胆汁淤积 (cholestasis) appears both as a clinical condition contraindicating specific drug use and as an adverse outcome of herbal alkaloid exposure. For example,沙库巴曲/缬沙坦 (sacubitril/valsartan) is explicitly contraindicated in patients with cholestatic liver disease (Child-Pugh C) [Data: Relationships (6667)]. Conversely, multiple alkaloids—including those from黄药子 and何首乌 species—induce cholestasis via MRP2 suppression, creating a feedback loop where drug-induced cholestasis could preclude future use of other therapeutics [Data: Entities (17107); Relationships (17486, 4935, 5014)]. This dual role highlights cholestasis as both a risk factor and